## Standard input and output

Each run handles **one** case. Read from standard input and print the result to standard output. Do not read a case count or print case numbers, prompts, or labels. Use the line structure shown in the test-case input below.

### Problem

The card game Duo uses cards that have 3 characteristics: a color, number, and symbol. The game starts with two face-up cards and 7 cards in the player’s hand. On a single turn, the player can choose one of the two face-up cards and match as many cards from their hand as possible, thus making a pile. A card can be placed on top of another card if exactly 2 of the characteristics match.

The ACSL cards use four colors (R=red, Y=yellow, G=green, B=blue), four numbers (1, 2, 3, 4), and four symbols (X=cross, O=circle, S=square, T=triangle). A card is represented by a 3-character string showing the color, number, and symbol, in that order. For example, the card R4S, shown on the left, is red with the number 4 and contains a square. There are 9 cards that could possibly be placed on top of this card: a red square with the number 1, 2, or 3; a yellow, green, or blue square with the number 4; a red cross, circle, or triangle with the number 4.

{{diagram:p-2604c282a8691ecd}}

{{diagram:p-3c22715601ec2cfb}}

The deck of cards may also have “wild” cards that have just 1 of these 3 characteristics and would be represented as a string such as a color (e.g. G) or a number (e.g. 3), or a shape (e.g. S). These cards can be matched if that one characteristic matches the other card’s same characteristic, regardless of what the other two characteristics are.

Your program will play multiple turns of Uno Duo for a single player. The turn begins with two face-up cards, 7 cards in the hand (think of them as being held from left to right), and a draw pile.

1. Find the leftmost card in your hand that matches one of the face-up cards. Place the matched card on that face-up card, creating a pile. If the card matches both face-up cards, choose the first card.

2. Try to match as many cards as you can in your hand on that pile. Each time, look at your cards starting at the leftmost card.

3. If a Wild Card is at the top of the pile, play the first card that matches it.

4. Your turn is over when there are no more cards in your hand or you cannot play any of them.

When your turn is over, add as many cards as possible from the draw pile so that you have no more than 7 cards in your hand. Continue with another turn until you've played all the cards in your hand and the draw pile is empty, or no cards can be matched. Output the number of cards in your hand, followed by the top card in each pile. Separate each by a single space.

### Example

### Input

```text
R4S B3O
R3X T B1T R1X B3X B1X Y4O
B4X Y3O B4T G2X B B3T
```

### Output

```text
3 R4S B3T
```

Explanation Initially, the two face-up cards and the 7 cards in the hand are as follows:

{{diagram:p-8cd082d7d031ea65}}

The first card in the hand that can be played is B3X (matching the color and the number, but not the shape) on the 2nd face-up card, thus making a pile. All future matches in this turn must go on the 2nd pile.

{{diagram:p-5ad33449475d01ce}}

The next moves are as follows:

{{diagram:p-a378387f264530ca}}

{{diagram:p-be06d12e12c36ccb}}

{{diagram:p-fa4473884e9738b4}}

{{diagram:p-6fcc4ecc004ddc50}}

{{diagram:p-ae3ad4aa46d7e259}}

At this point, the only card remaining in the hand is Y4O. It cannot be placed on Pile #2, so the turn ends. The hand needs to be replenished with 6 cards from the draw pile so the player’s hand is now: Y4O B4X Y3O B4T G2X B B3T On the next turn, choose Pile #2 with the card B4T. Then play B4X, B, and B3T in that order. Since the Draw Pile is empty, the game ends.

{{diagram:p-13adcb528967ede3}}

{{diagram:p-8d5f0b4da231cc13}}

{{diagram:p-eee806452b5dce0e}}

{{diagram:p-ce265eff4df1fc50}}

{{diagram:p-2e63f3862fb7d6e5}}

There are 3 unplayable cards left in the hand (Y4O, Y3O, and G2X) and the draw pile is empty. The top cards in the piles are R4S on Pile #1 and B3T on Pile #2. The output is the string: 3 R4S B3T.

### Input

There will be three strings: the 2 face-up cards; the 7 cards in the player’s hand; and the cards in the draw pile. There will be no more than 50 cards in the draw pile. All cards are separated by a single space.

### Output

Output a string containing the number of cards that are left in the player’s hand, the top card on Pile #1 and the top card on Pile #2, each separated by a single space.

